# vscode-extension/.vscodeignore
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What NOT to put inside the .vsix that gets sent to beta testers over WhatsApp,
# LinkedIn and email.
#
# There was no .vscodeignore before this file, and `vsce package` without one
# ships almost the whole folder: the TypeScript sources, the source maps, the
# tsconfig, and — the part that matters — `src/extension_backup.ts` and
# `src/fileOps_backup.ts`, superseded drafts that are kept on disk for reference
# and are not what the extension runs. A .vsix is an ordinary zip file; anyone
# who receives one can read every file in it. Shipping abandoned drafts of the
# file-access code to strangers is a poor way to introduce a security feature.
#
# Everything the extension needs at runtime is `out/*.js` plus package.json, so
# the rule is: exclude sources, keep compiled output, and exclude the compiled
# copies of the drafts too — `tsconfig.json` no longer compiles them, but stale
# `out/*_backup.js` from an earlier build can still be sitting there.

# TypeScript sources and build configuration
src/**
tsconfig.json
.vscode-test/**
**/*.ts
!out/**/*.d.ts

# Source maps: useful locally, 40% of the package, and a complete reconstruction
# of the sources this file exists to withhold.
out/**/*.js.map

# Compiled copies of the superseded drafts, in case an older build left them.
out/**/*_backup.js
out/**/*_claude.js
out/**/*_backup.js.map
out/**/*_claude.js.map

# Build metadata and previous packages
**/.gitignore
**/.eslintrc.json
package-lock.json
*.vsix
node_modules/.bin/**
